-- title:
--   Strongly polynomial rational linear programming on a real RAM (Smale's 9th problem)
-- statement:
--   This is OpenAI's formal strongly polynomial algorithm for rational linear programming, the optimization form of Smale's ninth problem.
--
--   The machine model, the input layout and the bit-length measure $\beta$ are those of the feasibility theorem `strongly_polynomial_feasibility`: a random-access machine with a rational scalar bank and a natural-number address bank, started with $m,d$ in address registers $0,1$ and with the entries of $A\in\mathbb Q^{m\times d}$ (row by row), then $b\in\mathbb Q^m$, then the objective $c\in\mathbb Q^d$ in its scalar registers. Write $L$ for the total bit length of this input. The theorem asserts that there are one program $P$ and constants $c_0,e_0,c_1,e_1\in\mathbb N$ such that for all $m,d\ge1$ and all $A,b,c$ the run of $P$ halts after at most $c_0(m+d+1)^{e_0}$ steps, every scalar register of every state has bit length at most $c_1(L+1)^{e_1}$, and the final state reports one of three outcomes:
--
--   1. tag $0$: a sparse Farkas certificate of infeasibility $y\ge0$, $A^{\mathsf T}y=0$, $b^{\mathsf T}y<0$, with at most $\operatorname{rank}A+1$ nonzero entries;
--   2. tag $1$: an optimal primal–dual pair: $x$ with $Ax\le b$, $y\ge0$ with $A^{\mathsf T}y=c$, and a value $v$ with
--   $$c^{\mathsf T}x=v=b^{\mathsf T}y\quad\text{and}\quad c^{\mathsf T}z\le v\ \text{ for every } z \text{ with } Az\le b;$$
--   3. tag $2$: unboundedness: a feasible point $x$ ($Ax\le b$) and a ray $v$ with $Av\le0$ and $c^{\mathsf T}v=1$.
--
--   Again the number of steps depends only on the dimensions, so linear programs over the rationals are solved in strongly polynomial time with polynomially bounded intermediate numbers.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `StronglyPolynomialOptimization` (module `OAI.Optimization.LinearProgramming.Specification`) packages this statement; the output layout is `OptimizationOutput` ($x$ in registers $0..d-1$, $y$ in $d..d+m-1$, the value in register $d+m$, the ray in $d..2d-1$). Proof transplanted from OpenAI's Lean formalization of strongly polynomial linear programming (github.com/openai/math, commit `3344da5`, Apache-2.0), backported from Lean 4.34.1 / Mathlib d13f23b to Lean 4.33.1 / Mathlib 0df444a.
